What you will learn: what interviewers and promotion committees expect at each level, in behaviour rather than job titles.

10
00:00:26.143 --> 00:00:28.340
The four levels map to this handbook's bands:

11
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.201
B1, Engineer, 0 to 3 years.

12
00:00:31.481 --> 00:00:34.233
B2, Senior engineer, 3 to 7 years.

13
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:37.351
B3, Staff or lead, 7 to 12 years.

14
00:00:37.631 --> 00:00:40.895
B4, Principal or architect, 12 plus years.

15
00:00:42.245 --> 00:00:45.829
Titles vary by company (VERIFY each company's ladder).

16
00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.605
The behaviours are remarkably consistent.

17
00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:56.714
The one lab answers a single question, handle the payment gateway's webhooks, at all four levels, against real PostgreSQL.

18
00:00:56.994 --> 00:01:07.447
5,000 payments with 13,475 deliveries, including duplicates, reordering, 178 lost events, two nodes and a restart:

19
00:01:07.727 --> 00:01:14.639
B1, In-memory dedup, 830, 747, 755, 104.

20
00:01:14.919 --> 00:01:22.834
B2, Database dedup in the same transaction, 0, 747, 755, 104.

21
00:01:23.114 --> 00:01:28.725
B3, + the domain's forward-only state machine, 0, 0, 82, 82.

22
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:35.088
B4, + reconciliation with the gateway, 0, 0, 0, 0.

23
00:01:35.368 --> 00:01:38.355
Each level is correct under one more kind of reality.

24
00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:39.616
Reading time:

25
00:01:39.896 --> 00:01:41.368
70 min + code.

26
00:01:42.718 --> 00:01:43.571
Part 1.

27
00:01:43.851 --> 00:01:45.771
Introduction and Why It Matters.

28
00:01:47.021 --> 00:01:50.691
A story: the promotion packet that said senior for the third year.

29
00:01:50.971 --> 00:01:54.533
Priya had fifteen years of experience and a strong track record.

30
00:01:54.813 --> 00:02:02.707
Her staff-promotion packet listed the systems she built: the payment integration, the search migration, the notification platform.

31
00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:05.760
The committee's feedback was the same for the third year:

32
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.576
Excellent senior engineer.

33
00:02:07.856 --> 00:02:09.840
Staff impact not demonstrated.

34
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.488
Her manager eventually translated it:

35
00:02:12.768 --> 00:02:14.688
Her packet described what she built.

36
00:02:16.038 --> 00:02:31.569
A staff packet describes which problems she found that nobody had assigned, which decisions she drove across teams, what she chose not to build, and how the organisation's systems got better because of her (standards, reviews, mentoring, incident follow-through).

37
00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:34.238
The same distinction appears in interviews.

38
00:02:34.518 --> 00:02:37.355
A senior candidate designs the system correctly.

39
00:02:37.635 --> 00:02:48.835
A staff candidate reframes the problem, argues the trade-offs, anticipates the failure modes that the organisation will actually hit, and makes the design operable and evolvable.

40
00:02:50.185 --> 00:02:54.175
Level is not years, and it is not how many technologies you can name.

41
00:02:54.455 --> 00:03:00.193
It is the scope and ambiguity you can handle, and the kinds of failure you design for without being asked.

42
00:03:01.543 --> 00:03:02.226
Quote:

43
00:03:02.506 --> 00:03:04.170
The thesis of this chapter.

44
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:10.146
Levels differ less in what you know than in what you anticipate and how far your influence reaches:

45
00:03:10.426 --> 00:03:11.791
B1 makes it work.

46
00:03:12.071 --> 00:03:14.973
B2 makes it work under concurrency and load.

47
00:03:15.253 --> 00:03:20.074
B3 makes it correct under the domain's real failure modes, and owns the trade-offs.

48
00:03:20.354 --> 00:03:28.845
B4 makes it correct under loss and change, across teams and years: reconciliation, evolution, operability, organisation.

49
00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:31.301
The lab shows the ladder on one problem.

