131
00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:19.917
Part 2.

132
00:06:20.197 --> 00:06:22.074
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

133
00:06:23.324 --> 00:06:25.863
Intuition: four drivers on the same road.

134
00:06:26.143 --> 00:06:28.383
A learner drives safely on a clear road.

135
00:06:28.663 --> 00:06:31.777
An experienced driver handles traffic and bad weather.

136
00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:38.457
A professional driver knows the route's hazards (the blind curve, the flooding underpass) and plans around them.

137
00:06:38.737 --> 00:06:46.033
A fleet manager ensures a hundred drivers are safe for years: routes, maintenance, training, incident reviews, cost.

138
00:06:46.313 --> 00:06:49.556
Same road, different scope of what they anticipate and own.

139
00:06:50.906 --> 00:06:51.738
B1:

140
00:06:52.018 --> 00:06:52.914
Engineer.

141
00:06:53.194 --> 00:06:59.658
Expectation: implements well-defined components correctly, with tests, and asks for help appropriately.

142
00:06:59.938 --> 00:07:09.303
In a design interview (rare at this level): produces a reasonable single-service design, knows basic storage and caching, and handles the happy path.

143
00:07:09.583 --> 00:07:16.709
In the lab: an in-memory dedup set works in a demo, and silently double-credits once there are two nodes or a restart.

144
00:07:16.989 --> 00:07:21.874
Growth edge: think about what happens when there are two of everything, and when things restart.

145
00:07:23.224 --> 00:07:24.099
B2:

146
00:07:24.379 --> 00:07:25.552
Senior engineer.

147
00:07:25.832 --> 00:07:30.312
Expectation: owns a service or feature end to end, independently.

148
00:07:30.592 --> 00:07:37.632
It is correct under concurrency and load, well designed at the API and data level, and tested and monitored.

149
00:07:37.912 --> 00:07:47.149
In a design interview: clarifies requirements;. estimates;. gives a clean API and data model;. produces a coherent high-level design;.

150
00:07:48.499 --> 00:07:54.259
delivers one solid deep dive with atomic operations and idempotency;. names trade-offs.

151
00:07:54.539 --> 00:08:00.598
In the lab: dedup in the database, in the same transaction as the effect, makes duplicates harmless.

152
00:08:00.878 --> 00:08:03.203
It still trusts arrival order and delivery.

153
00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:10.630
Growth edge: model the domain's state machine and its real-world failure modes, and own decisions beyond your service.

154
00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:12.876
B3:

155
00:08:13.156 --> 00:08:14.265
Staff engineer.

156
00:08:14.545 --> 00:08:18.108
Expectation: owns a system or domain across teams.

157
00:08:18.388 --> 00:08:20.927
Defines problems as well as solving them.

158
00:08:21.207 --> 00:08:25.985
Makes and documents the decisions others build on (ADRs, design docs).

159
00:08:26.265 --> 00:08:28.164
Raises the bar for other seniors.

160
00:08:28.444 --> 00:08:36.359
In a design interview: drives the whole session;. chooses the deep dive where the risk is;. argues trade-offs with flip conditions;.

161
00:08:37.709 --> 00:08:46.946
treats failure handling (state machines, sagas, degradation, SLOs) as part of the design;. discusses operability and cost unprompted.

162
00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:54.095
In the lab: encodes the domain rule that a payment's status only moves forward, so reordered events can't corrupt it.

163
00:08:54.375 --> 00:08:56.189
Side effects go to an outbox.

164
00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:57.962
The remaining gap is loss.

165
00:08:58.242 --> 00:09:03.085
Growth edge: assume every channel lies eventually, and design the correction loop.

166
00:09:03.365 --> 00:09:05.391
Think in years and organisations.

167
00:09:06.741 --> 00:09:07.637
B4:

168
00:09:07.917 --> 00:09:09.411
Principal or architect.

169
00:09:09.691 --> 00:09:13.147
Expectation: sets technical direction across domains.

170
00:09:13.427 --> 00:09:16.264
Designs for loss, change and scale over years.

171
00:09:16.544 --> 00:09:19.979
Aligns architecture with organisation and economics.

172
00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:22.968
Shapes standards, the hiring bar and culture.

173
00:09:23.248 --> 00:09:36.304
In a design interview: reframes the prompt (what's the business goal? what's the smallest thing that works?);. phases the design (now, next year, at 10 times );. designs the correction loops (reconciliation, audits);.

174
00:09:37.654 --> 00:09:44.054
quantifies reliability and cost;. addresses migration and team structure;. knows what not to build.

175
00:09:44.334 --> 00:09:52.590
In the lab: accepts that at-least-once channels can still lose messages, and closes the loop with reconciliation against the source of truth.

176
00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:54.598
Every invariant reaches zero.

177
00:09:54.878 --> 00:09:56.947
Growth edge: none that is technical.

178
00:09:57.227 --> 00:10:00.577
It is judgement, communication, and multiplying others.

