367
00:18:55.839 --> 00:18:56.927
What's real here.

368
00:18:57.207 --> 00:19:06.530
One program implements all four levels against a real PostgreSQL: real transactions, unique constraints, guarded updates and an outbox.

369
00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:11.845
It uses two concurrent consumer threads, one of which restarts by losing its memory.

370
00:19:12.125 --> 00:19:17.629
The gateway's truth, the duplicates, the reordering and the losses are generated with a fixed seed.

371
00:19:17.909 --> 00:19:23.541
The duplicate count for B1 varies slightly between runs, because thread interleaving differs.

372
00:19:24.891 --> 00:19:25.787
The program:

373
00:19:27.137 --> 00:19:30.209
Here is the Java code, WebhookLevelsL.java.

374
00:19:30.489 --> 00:19:34.393
It runs to 180 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

375
00:19:34.673 --> 00:19:54.086
WebhookLevelsL.java, run with (PostgreSQL on:5581, db shop): java -Duser.timezone=UTC -cp or tmp or eslab or postgresql-42.7.4.jar WebhookLevelsL.java (JDK 21).

376
00:19:54.366 --> 00:19:56.542
ONE QUESTION, FOUR LEVELS OF ANSWER:

377
00:19:56.822 --> 00:19:58.934
Handle the payment gateway's webhooks.

378
00:19:59.214 --> 00:20:01.753
5,000 payments; the gateway sends.

379
00:20:04.603 --> 00:20:05.520
On screen:

380
00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:25.853
5,000 payments, 13,475 webhook deliveries (25% duplicated, 15% of payments out of. order, 178 events never delivered); 2 nodes, one restart. answer effects applied status went final status money effects. twice backwards. wrong. missing.

381
00:20:26.133 --> 00:20:35.008
B1) in-memory seen-set per node, apply status 830 747 755 104.

382
00:20:35.288 --> 00:20:44.504
B2) dedup by event id in the same DB transaction 0 747 755 104.

383
00:20:44.784 --> 00:20:50.565
B3) B2 + forward-only status + outbox 0 0 82 82.

384
00:20:50.845 --> 00:21:02.323
B4) B3 + reconciliation with the gateway's list API 0 0 0 0. then every answer 'handles webhooks'; they differ in which failure modes they have.

385
00:21:02.603 --> 00:21:04.117
The rest is in the chapter.

386
00:21:06.967 --> 00:21:09.805
L1, The B1 answer: in-memory dedup.

387
00:21:10.085 --> 00:21:11.023
Requirement:

388
00:21:11.303 --> 00:21:13.053
Don't process a webhook twice.

389
00:21:13.333 --> 00:21:14.186
Reading it:

390
00:21:14.466 --> 00:21:16.813
It works on one node until it restarts.

391
00:21:17.093 --> 00:21:21.658
With two nodes, and one restarting, 830 effects were applied twice.

392
00:21:21.938 --> 00:21:28.509
It also overwrites status in arrival order, so 747 payments moved backwards at some point.

393
00:21:28.789 --> 00:21:32.821
The 2% lost events leave 104 money effects missing.

394
00:21:34.171 --> 00:21:37.477
L2, The B2 answer: dedup in the same transaction.

395
00:21:37.757 --> 00:21:38.696
Requirement:

396
00:21:38.976 --> 00:21:42.283
Duplicates must be harmless across nodes and restarts.

397
00:21:42.563 --> 00:21:43.416
Reading it:

398
00:21:43.696 --> 00:21:50.437
The event-id primary key, inserted in the same transaction as the effect, makes every duplicate a no-op:

399
00:21:50.717 --> 00:21:52.061
0 double effects.

400
00:21:52.341 --> 00:21:54.048
Ordering and loss are untouched.

401
00:21:54.328 --> 00:22:00.451
The status still goes backwards, and final statuses are still wrong for 755 payments.

402
00:22:01.801 --> 00:22:04.958
L3, The B3 answer: the domain's state machine.

403
00:22:05.238 --> 00:22:06.177
Requirement:

404
00:22:06.457 --> 00:22:09.294
A late event must never move a payment backwards.

405
00:22:09.574 --> 00:22:10.427
Reading it:

406
00:22:10.707 --> 00:22:15.209
The guarded UPDATE (WHERE status new) encodes AUTHORIZED CAPTURED REFUNDED:

407
00:22:15.489 --> 00:22:16.769
0 regressions.

408
00:22:17.049 --> 00:22:19.865
Effects go to an outbox in the same transaction.

409
00:22:20.145 --> 00:22:23.622
A refund implies the capture happened, so its credit is owed.

410
00:22:23.902 --> 00:22:26.334
The domain knowledge recovers some losses.

411
00:22:26.614 --> 00:22:28.385
82 payments remain wrong.

412
00:22:28.665 --> 00:22:30.670
Their final event never arrived.

413
00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:34.793
L4, The B4 answer: reconciliation.

414
00:22:35.073 --> 00:22:36.012
Requirement:

415
00:22:36.292 --> 00:22:40.068
Our records must match the gateway's, even when messages are lost.

416
00:22:40.348 --> 00:22:41.201
Reading it:

417
00:22:41.481 --> 00:22:49.012
Comparing against the gateway's list API and repairing through the same forward-only path brings every invariant to 0.

418
00:22:49.292 --> 00:22:51.532
In production, add metrics on drift.

419
00:22:51.812 --> 00:22:58.895
A sudden jump in drift is a systemic bug, not noise, so page and stop auto-repair (Ch 45).

